---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";

const guide = getGuide("agent-skill");
---

<DocsLayout guide={guide}>
<section class="docs-hero docs-searchable" id="overview" data-search="agent skill optional instructions codex unfamiliar repository scan read only">
          <p class="docs-kicker">Optional agent skills</p>
          <h1>Make the safe first move.<span>Before code runs.</span></h1>
          <p>
            The repyy agent skill gives a coding agent a repeatable instruction set for preflighting unfamiliar repositories. It asks the agent to scan first,
            preserve the target as data, explain the evidence, and report unresolved coverage. When the CLI is unavailable, it continues with a limited static review.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-summary" aria-label="Agent skill summary">
            <span><b>Instructions</b>Changes agent behavior</span><span><b>Separate</b>Does not install the CLI</span
            ><span><b>Private</b>Reports stay local</span>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-callout">
            <strong>Install the skill and CLI separately</strong>
            <p>The skill is guidance for an agent. Scanner results require a trusted repyy executable; manual review remains available without one.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="install" data-search="install skill npx skills add codex global interactive command">
          <h2>Install the skill</h2>
          <p>
            Run one of these commands in your terminal. The interactive form lets the skills tool prompt you; the global form installs instructions for Codex
            without prompts.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div><span>Interactive install</span><button type="button" data-copy="npx skills add Kevin-Umali/repyy --skill repyy">Copy</button></div>
            <code>npx skills add Kevin-Umali/repyy --skill repyy</code>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div><span>Codex, global</span><button type="button" data-copy="npx skills add Kevin-Umali/repyy --skill repyy -g -a codex -y">Copy</button></div>
            <code>npx skills add Kevin-Umali/repyy --skill repyy -g -a codex -y</code>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Before you run it</strong>
            <p>
              Install Node.js and npm so <code>npx</code> is available, install Git and keep it on <code>PATH</code>, and allow network access to fetch the
              published skill. Git and network are needed for this skill installation, even though a local repyy scan needs neither.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p>
            Neither command installs dependencies from a repository you plan to scan. To use the scanner, install repyy separately using the
            <a href="../installation/">installation guide</a>, then confirm it with <code>repyy version</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Install a helper from the same repository with <code>npx skills add Kevin-Umali/repyy --skill NAME</code>, replacing
            <code>NAME</code> with the skill you need. The main skill recommends helpers when useful and continues if one is missing.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-table compact" role="table" aria-label="Repyy helper skills">
            <div role="row"><b role="rowheader">repyy-explain</b><span role="cell">Explain a report and check selected findings against the matching source.</span></div>
            <div role="row"><b role="rowheader">repyy-review</b><span role="cell">Trace a suspicious path or scan gap manually, including without the CLI.</span></div>
            <div role="row"><b role="rowheader">repyy-intel</b><span role="cell">Check a package/version or file hash against its sourced indicator.</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            All four skills use the host's built-in question tool when available to ask for missing targets, reports, or goals.
            They use inputs already supplied and do not install missing tools or execute the target.
          </p>
          <p>
            Scanner targets are directories or supported Git URLs. For an individual artifact, the skill stages only that approved regular file in a
            private directory and scans the directory without extracting or executing it. It does not expand the review to a broad parent folder.
            A single skill file leaves missing scripts and references outside the review.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section
          class="docs-section docs-searchable"
          id="workflow"
          data-search="workflow scan json report html rules intel incomplete verdict no findings review required do not run"
        >
          <h2>What the skill tells an agent to do</h2>
          <ol class="docs-list">
            <li>Use the target and goal already supplied; ask for missing inputs with the built-in question tool when available.</li>
            <li>Check the trusted installed scanner, intelligence snapshot age, and rule provenance offline; use limited manual review if the CLI is unavailable.</li>
            <li>Scan every requested target together where possible and save bounded JSON.</li>
            <li>Render a local HTML report from that JSON without rescanning source.</li>
            <li>Validate important findings against surrounding source and trace suspicious inputs toward execution or data destinations.</li>
            <li>Report coverage, findings, locations, context, confidence, counterevidence and remediation without granting execution permission.</li>
            <li>Recommend a report, manual-review, or intelligence helper when useful, and continue without installing it if it is unavailable.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>Recommended first pass</span><button type="button" data-copy="repyy scan --format json --output repyy.scan.json TARGET...">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <code>repyy scan --format json --output repyy.scan.json TARGET...</code>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>Reviewable offline report</span
              ><button type="button" data-copy="repyy report repyy.scan.json --format html --output repyy.report.html">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <code>repyy report repyy.scan.json --format html --output repyy.report.html</code>
          </div>
          <p>
            The agent should treat <code>SCAN INCOMPLETE</code> as unresolved and state that <code>NO FINDINGS</code> is not proof of safety. A manual review must not
            invent a scanner verdict. It should not
            paste credential-shaped evidence into chat.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="docker" data-search="docker sandbox digest signed image remote https ssh keep workdir isolation">
          <h2>Use the opt-in Docker backend</h2>
          <p>
            The skill includes an opt-in Docker path for local folders and HTTPS remotes. Prepare Docker and the exact signed, digest-pinned image for the
            installed repyy release before asking the agent to scan.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>Docker scan</span
              ><button type="button" data-copy="repyy scan TARGET --sandbox=docker --format html --output repyy.report.html">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <code>repyy scan TARGET --sandbox=docker --format html --output repyy.report.html</code>
          </div>
          <p>
            Docker rejects SSH URLs and <code>--keep-workdir</code>. Missing Docker or a missing release-matched image is a preflight error; the skill must
            never accept a silent host fallback. Read the <a href="../isolation/">isolation guide</a> for Docker setup and image verification.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section
          class="docs-section docs-searchable"
          id="limits"
          data-search="safety privacy limitations target execute build test dependency upload telemetry intel update rollback remote shallow"
        >
          <h2>Understand the limits</h2>
          <div class="docs-table compact" role="table" aria-label="Agent skill boundaries">
            <div role="row">
              <b role="rowheader">Target code</b
              ><span role="cell">The agent must not run, import, evaluate, build, test, or install target dependencies before review.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="rowheader">Network</b
              ><span role="cell">Local scans need no network. Remote host scans use Git; Docker separates HTTPS fetch from network-disabled analysis.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="rowheader">Reports</b
              ><span role="cell">HTML reports are local artifacts. They make no requests but still contain paths and redacted evidence.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="rowheader">Intelligence</b
              ><span role="cell">Updates are explicit. The agent should not run <code>repyy intel update</code> unless the user asks.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The skill is a workflow guardrail, not a guarantee that a repository is safe. A human still decides whether to execute code, install dependencies,
            or continue in an isolated environment.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Read the source instructions</strong>
            <p>
              The
              <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/blob/main/skills/repyy/SKILL.md">repository copy of the skill ↗</a>
              is the canonical reference for agent behavior. Keep it aligned with the installed CLI and review it when upgrading repyy.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
</DocsLayout>
